    <li><a href="https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIST, AI Risk Management Framework</a>, 26 January 2023</li>
    <li><a href="https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIST AI 600-1, Generative AI Profile</a>, July 2024</li>
    <li>Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 and the Digital Omnibus on AI, Official Journal of the European Union, 24 July 2026 (as described in the law-firm sources above)</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="mb-12 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-6" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 space-y-6">
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">Does the EU AI Act apply to a business outside the EU?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">It can. Law-firm summaries describe two routes: placing an AI system on the EU market, including software used by EU customers, and cases where the output of an AI system is used in the EU. Whether it applies to you depends on the facts, so ask a qualified lawyer.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">Which EU AI Act rules apply now?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">As reported in October 2026, the Article 50 transparency duties have applied since 2 August 2026, for example telling people when they are interacting with an AI system. The heavier high-risk obligations were delayed to 2 December 2027 and 2 August 2028.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">If a contract promises data protection, is the data safe from foreign authorities?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">Not entirely. Canada's privacy regulator says no contract can override the criminal, national security or any other laws of the country to which the information has been transferred. A contract is still needed, but where data is processed remains a risk factor.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">Is this ebook legal advice?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">No. It summarises published regulator and law-firm sources as of the dates shown in the Sources section. Laws change, so confirm each point with a qualified lawyer in your market.</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

